nihlak = (i:1) (t:phrase) (f:everyday) (w:among two/of the two/between two) (d:<nih>, two, and <lak>, among, written as one word; {nihlak ah} is of the two, {nihlak pan} out of the two) (j:nih/lak) (r:zd:21989/zd:21918) (q:draft)
nihmah = (i:1) (t:pron) (f:everyday) (w:both/the two) (d:<nih> and the emphatic <mah> written as one word: the two of them, both) (j:nih/mah) (r:zd:16976/zd:20142) (q:draft)
nihpen = (i:1) (t:phrase) (f:everyday) (w:two of them/the two) (d:<nih>, two, with the topic marker <pen>, written as one word: the two, or two of those just named; {asite lakah nihpen} is two among the dead) (j:nih/pen) (r:zd:11752/zd:15123) (q:draft)
nihtak = (i:1) (t:num) (f:everyday) (w:two/both/as many as two) (d:<nih>, two, and <tak> written as one word after a noun: two of them, with weight on the number; {nihtak in} is the two of them together as doers) (j:nih/tak) (r:zd:11504/zd:17514) (q:draft)
nihtawh = (i:1) (t:phrase) (f:everyday) (w:with two/by two) (d:<nih>, two, and <tawh>, with, written as one word after a noun: with two of them, by means of two) (j:nih/tawh) (r:zd:12456/zd:11545/zd:14848) (q:draft)
nihtepen = (i:1) (t:num) (f:everyday) (w:the two/both) (d:<nihte>, the two, with the topic marker <pen>, written as one word after a noun: the two of them, as what the sentence is about) (j:nihte/pen) (r:zd:14899/zd:16584) (q:draft)
nihtezong = (i:1) (t:phrase) (f:everyday) (w:both also/both of them too) (d:<nihte>, the two, and <zong>, also, written as one word after a noun: both of them, the two as well) (j:nihte/zong) (r:zd:14965/zd:15994) (q:draft)
nihtuak = (i:1) (t:pron) (f:everyday) (w:both/both of them) (d:<nih>, two, and <tuak>, each, written as one word: {a nihtuak} is both of the two) (j:nih/tuak) (r:zd:22495/zd:23590/zd:26953) (q:draft)
nihveitak = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:time/everyday) (w:twice/as many as two times) (d:<nihvei>, twice, with the emphatic <tak>, written as one word: two whole times, stressing that it happened more than once) (j:nihvei/tak) (r:zd:11285/zd:13260) (q:draft)
nihzong = (i:1) (t:phrase) (f:everyday) (w:both/the two also) (d:<nih> and <zong> written as one word, after a noun: both of them, the two as well) (j:nih/zong) (r:zd:11234/zd:15577/zd:15255) (q:draft)
nii = (i:1) (t:n) (f:time) (j:ni) (r:zd:24496/zd:22470) (q:draft)
niik = (i:1) (t:n) (f:home) (j:nik) (r:zd:9254/zd:24123) (q:draft)
niin = (i:2) (t:adj) (f:everyday/home/nature) (w:dirty/filthy/unclean) (d:a spelling of <nin>, dirty, with a long vowel, not the day word {ni-in}: of water, people or words, dirty; {kam niin} is foul language) (s:nin) (r:zd:23551/zd:21174) (q:draft)
niin = (i:3) (t:n) (f:nature/health/home) (w:dirt/filth/waste) (d:{niin} as a noun: dirt and waste, what people throw away or pass from the body; {huih niin} is dirt in the air, pollution) (r:zd:22650) (q:draft, low confidence)
nikbawk = (i:1) (t:n) (f:home) (w:skirt/dress) (d:a skirt or dress worn by women; {nikbawk tom} is a short skirt) (s:nik) (r:zd:10823/zd:19707/zd:20501) (q:draft)
nikbawknuai = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:home) (w:under the skirt) (d:<nikbawk>, skirt, and <nuai>, under, written as one word) (r:zd:16717) (q:draft, low confidence)
nikhatin = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:time) (w:per day/a day/in one day) (d:<nikhat>, one day, with <in> written as one word: in one day, each day) (j:nikhat/in) (r:zd:18089/zd:19734/zd:19213) (q:draft)
nikhatni = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:time) (w:one day/some day) (d:<nikhat> and <ni> written as one word: on a certain day, in a story, or with <ciangin> some day in the future; after the name of a month or feast it is the first day) (j:nikhat/ni) (r:zd:21876/zd:20218/zd:10729) (q:draft)
nikhatsung = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:time) (w:in one day/within a day) (d:<nikhat>, one day, with <sung>, within: in the course of a single day, mostly as {nikhatsung in}) (j:nikhat/sung) (r:zd:17917/zd:19786/zd:14865) (q:draft)
niklepuan = (i:1) (t:n) (f:home) (w:clothes/clothing) (d:<nik>, skirt, <le>, and, <puan>, cloth, written as one word: clothes of every kind) (j:nik/le/puan) (r:zd:22650/zd:15851) (q:draft)
niknek = (i:1) (t:adv) (f:everyday) (w:limply/softly/droopingly) (d:descriptive word placed after a verb: soft, limp or drooping, as a thing that wilts, a body worn out, or a gentle smile) (r:zd:23419/zd:21463) (q:draft, low confidence)
